empty streets in nepal's capital as a  two-week lockdown is imposed to limit  the spread of coronavirus  it's the second wave to hit the country  infections have surged and more than  half are in the capital kathmandu  hospitals are seeing more cases in a  shorter span of time  the second wave is more potent with more  young people and children infected  as health workers struggle to contain  the growing number of patients  experts fear that thousands may have  caught the mutant variant strains  emerging out of neighbouring india  thousands of workers from nepal have  left their jobs in indian cities  where korvid-19 is spiraling out of  control they're arriving here in  nepalese towns by the border and that's  creating a major health crisis  it's like a mini india if the situation  was similar in the capital which has a  dense population  it would be difficult to cope with the  existing health infrastructure  more than 300 000 people in nepal have  got covet 19.  90 have recovered over 3 000 people have  died  as daily cases surge experts warn that  the epidemic  could overwhelm nepal's fragile health  system if the country is unable to break  the chain of transmission in time  nepal's health authorities have  repeatedly appealed to the public to  wear masks  avoid crowds and to maintain social  distance but recent political rallies  and religious festivals  have helped fuel the spread of the  disease thousands rushed to leave the  capital after the lockdown was announced  i'm waiting at the bus stop for a seat  out of kathmandu  it's crowded the situation is difficult  i'd rather be back in my village  where i can make ends meet health  authorities fear this exodus  could spread the virus in villages  across nepal keep them  out of control the situation is not out  of control  but kovet is spreading and cases are  rising we are prioritizing our efforts  to contain kovid emergencies  nepal launched a vaccination campaign in  january giving doses from india and  china to nearly 2 million people  but with a population of 28 million and  limited access to vaccines  nepal will have to seek support from the  international community  to prevent the epidemic from spiraling  out of control  ramyata limbu al-jazeera kathmandu
 

